WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.325
Lesson 1.5.

00:00:02.325 --> 00:00:04.580
Stablecoins and how they break.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:06.352
By the end, you'll be able to

00:00:06.352 --> 00:00:08.891
classify any stablecoin by its design, and

00:00:08.891 --> 00:00:11.135
name exactly what could break its peg.

00:00:12.435 --> 00:00:15.076
Here's the plan, and we'll walk through every part

00:00:15.076 --> 00:00:17.395
of it visually, not just talk about it.

00:00:17.395 --> 00:00:20.190
First, the four designs, side by side.

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:23.036
Second, the arbitrage mechanism that holds a peg

00:00:23.036 --> 00:00:25.485
together, as a loop you'll watch run.

00:00:25.485 --> 00:00:29.274
Third, two real, dated collapses: Terra in

00:00:29.274 --> 00:00:32.182
2022, and USDC in 2023, each one

00:00:32.182 --> 00:00:34.950
walked through step by step.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:38.598
And finally, a full worked example with real percentages.

00:00:39.898 --> 00:00:40.883
Four designs.

00:00:42.183 --> 00:00:45.358
Three designs, and exactly how each one breaks.

00:00:45.358 --> 00:00:47.977
Fiat-backed: cash and treasuries held by an

00:00:47.977 --> 00:00:50.358
issuer, and it breaks if those reserves

00:00:50.358 --> 00:00:52.503
are frozen or turn out missing.

00:00:52.503 --> 00:00:57.098
Crypto-backed: over-collateralised with on-chain crypto, and it breaks if

00:00:57.098 --> 00:01:00.813
collateral falls faster than liquidations can catch up.

00:01:00.813 --> 00:01:03.946
Algorithmic: held up mostly by incentives and a

00:01:03.946 --> 00:01:07.210
sister token, and it breaks the moment confidence

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:10.213
goes and the mechanism spirals against itself.

00:01:11.513 --> 00:01:14.418
And the fourth design: synthetic, or hedged.

00:01:14.418 --> 00:01:17.115
This one holds a spot asset and a matching

00:01:17.115 --> 00:01:20.126
short position on a derivative, so the two move

00:01:20.126 --> 00:01:23.123
opposite each other and roughly cancel out.

00:01:23.123 --> 00:01:26.264
It breaks when funding on that short turns sharply

00:01:26.264 --> 00:01:29.590
negative for a sustained period, or when the exchange

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:32.723
holding the position runs into trouble itself.

00:01:32.723 --> 00:01:35.811
Four designs, four different failure points, and none

00:01:35.811 --> 00:01:37.698
of them fail for the same reason.

00:01:38.998 --> 00:01:40.511
One real number for scale,

00:01:40.511 --> 00:01:42.493
so this doesn't feel abstract.

00:01:42.493 --> 00:01:46.108
Stablecoins collectively represent well over a hundred

00:01:46.108 --> 00:01:49.131
billion dollars of value, moving through DeFi

00:01:49.131 --> 00:01:51.468
and exchanges every single day.

00:01:51.468 --> 00:01:53.447
That's the size of the thing whose

00:01:53.447 --> 00:01:55.709
design you're now able to classify, and

00:01:55.709 --> 00:01:57.914
whose failure points you can now name,

00:01:57.914 --> 00:01:59.837
before you deposit into any of it.

00:02:01.137 --> 00:02:02.227
Quick check.

00:02:02.227 --> 00:02:04.178
Two stablecoins are both backed

00:02:04.178 --> 00:02:06.247
by the exact same collateral.

00:02:06.247 --> 00:02:08.482
Are they diversified, if you hold both?

00:02:12.902 --> 00:02:15.505
The answer: no. They share the same failure

00:02:15.505 --> 00:02:18.226
point, so a problem with that collateral hits

00:02:18.226 --> 00:02:20.297
both at once, not just one of them.

00:02:21.597 --> 00:02:22.792
The peg mechanism.

00:02:24.092 --> 00:02:25.984
Here's the loop that holds a peg

00:02:25.984 --> 00:02:28.462
near one dollar, running in real time.

00:02:28.462 --> 00:02:30.864
The coin trades at ninety-eight cents on

00:02:30.864 --> 00:02:33.567
the open market, below its dollar target.

00:02:33.567 --> 00:02:36.912
An arbitrageur buys it cheap, at ninety-eight cents.

00:02:36.912 --> 00:02:38.114
They redeem it with the

00:02:38.114 --> 00:02:39.717
issuer, for a full dollar.

00:02:39.717 --> 00:02:42.645
And they pocket the two-cent difference, which pushes

00:02:42.645 --> 00:02:45.248
buying pressure back into the market and nudges

00:02:45.248 --> 00:02:47.337
the price back toward one dollar.

00:02:47.337 --> 00:02:50.253
That loop runs constantly, automatically, as

00:02:50.253 --> 00:02:52.457
long as redemption actually works.

00:02:53.757 --> 00:02:56.744
Now run the exact same loop backwards, above one

00:02:56.744 --> 00:03:00.030
dollar, because the peg works in both directions.

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:03.055
The coin trades at one dollar two, above target.

00:03:03.055 --> 00:03:05.195
An arbitrageur mints new coins from

00:03:05.195 --> 00:03:07.575
the issuer, paying one dollar each.

00:03:07.575 --> 00:03:09.501
They sell those new coins on the

00:03:09.501 --> 00:03:11.610
open market, at one dollar two.

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:13.748
And that new supply pushes the price

00:03:13.748 --> 00:03:16.070
back down, toward one dollar again.

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:19.081
Above or below target, the same arbitrage pressure

00:03:19.081 --> 00:03:20.970
pulls the price back to the peg.

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:24.476
Read both loops again, and notice the

00:03:24.476 --> 00:03:27.215
one word that makes them work: redemption.

00:03:27.215 --> 00:03:29.073
The peg isn't magic, and it isn't

00:03:29.073 --> 00:03:31.340
really about the price chart at all.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:32.765
It's only as strong as

00:03:32.765 --> 00:03:35.420
redemption access and reserve quality.

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:38.080
Break either one, and the loop simply stops running.

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:42.240
Case study: Terra, May 2022.

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:46.575
Here's how it actually unwound, step by step.

00:03:46.575 --> 00:03:49.791
U.S.T. depended on confidence in its sister

00:03:49.791 --> 00:03:52.350
token, Luna, to hold its peg, with

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:54.550
no hard reserve behind it.

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:58.605
Large withdrawals began, pushing U.S.T. below one dollar.

00:03:58.605 --> 00:04:01.583
The mechanism tried to defend the peg by minting

00:04:01.583 --> 00:04:05.105
huge amounts of Luna, which crashed Luna's own price.

00:04:05.105 --> 00:04:07.809
A falling Luna made the peg mechanism weaker,

00:04:07.809 --> 00:04:11.225
not stronger, which triggered still more withdrawals.

00:04:11.225 --> 00:04:13.919
And the two tokens spiralled down together,

00:04:13.919 --> 00:04:15.940
toward zero, in a matter of days.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:19.800
Put a real number on how fast it happened.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.311
UST traded near $1 for most of its

00:04:22.311 --> 00:04:25.539
history, and within roughly five days in May

00:04:25.539 --> 00:04:28.409
2022, it and Luna together lost tens of

00:04:28.409 --> 00:04:31.435
billions of dollars of combined value.

00:04:31.435 --> 00:04:33.215
That's not a slow decline you'd

00:04:33.215 --> 00:04:34.795
have weeks to react to.

00:04:34.795 --> 00:04:37.392
It's the specific speed a reflexive spiral

00:04:37.392 --> 00:04:39.445
moves at, once it actually starts.

00:04:40.745 --> 00:04:42.695
This is what the checklist means

00:04:42.695 --> 00:04:44.650
by a reflexive death spiral.

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:46.884
Each step made the next step worse, not

00:04:46.884 --> 00:04:49.676
better, and there was no outside reserve anywhere

00:04:49.676 --> 00:04:51.260
in the loop to stop it.

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:54.080
That's the specific, structural weakness of an

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:56.660
algorithmic design, not a one-off accident.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:01.170
Case study: USDC, March 2023.

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:05.315
Here's the shape of it, as it actually happened.

00:05:05.315 --> 00:05:07.348
Through most of March, USDC

00:05:07.348 --> 00:05:09.680
held steady near one dollar.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:11.994
Then, on March 11th, news broke that

00:05:11.994 --> 00:05:14.246
part of its reserves sat at Silicon

00:05:14.246 --> 00:05:16.735
Valley Bank, which had just failed.

00:05:16.735 --> 00:05:18.945
The price dropped sharply, down

00:05:18.945 --> 00:05:21.040
toward $0.87 at its worst.

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:25.635
Once regulators confirmed depositors, including Circle's reserves, would

00:05:25.635 --> 00:05:28.657
be made whole, the price recovered fully within

00:05:28.657 --> 00:05:30.545
about two days, back to $1.00.

00:05:31.845 --> 00:05:33.949
Zoom into just the two recovery days,

00:05:33.949 --> 00:05:36.575
because that's where the real lesson sits.

00:05:36.575 --> 00:05:39.022
On March eleventh, the discount was near

00:05:39.022 --> 00:05:41.649
its worst, close to eighty-seven cents, and

00:05:41.649 --> 00:05:44.575
headlines were uncertain about the reserves.

00:05:44.575 --> 00:05:48.723
By March thirteenth, regulators had confirmed depositors would be

00:05:48.723 --> 00:05:52.808
made whole, redemption confidence returned, and the price closed

00:05:52.808 --> 00:05:56.125
the gap almost completely, back near one dollar.

00:05:56.125 --> 00:05:59.006
Two days, from worst point to fully recovered,

00:05:59.006 --> 00:06:02.010
once the actual question, “are the reserves real

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:04.155
and reachable,” got a clear answer.

00:06:05.455 --> 00:06:08.015
Compare the two collapses directly, because

00:06:08.015 --> 00:06:10.235
the contrast is the whole lesson.

00:06:10.235 --> 00:06:13.152
Terra had no outside reserve, so the spiral had

00:06:13.152 --> 00:06:16.065
nothing to stop it, and it never recovered.

00:06:16.065 --> 00:06:19.589
U.S.D.C. had real reserves, just temporarily hard

00:06:19.589 --> 00:06:22.407
to access, so once access was restored,

00:06:22.407 --> 00:06:24.610
the peg came straight back.

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:27.863
Same word, “depeg,” two completely different outcomes,

00:06:27.863 --> 00:06:30.465
because the underlying design was different.

00:06:31.765 --> 00:06:32.855
Quick check.

00:06:32.855 --> 00:06:35.030
What actually caused USDC's price

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:36.630
to recover in March 2023?

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:44.377
[[pause 4]] The answer: confirmation that the reserves were

00:06:44.377 --> 00:06:47.483
real and depositors would be made whole, which restored

00:06:47.483 --> 00:06:51.110
confidence and let redemption function normally again. Not a

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:53.535
change to how the coin itself works.

00:06:54.835 --> 00:06:55.840
Worked example.

00:06:57.140 --> 00:06:59.870
Here's the arbitrage math, worked in full.

00:06:59.870 --> 00:07:03.280
Buy the coin on the open market for $0.97.

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:06.845
Redeem it with the issuer for $1.00,

00:07:06.845 --> 00:07:10.420
minus a 0.1% fee, so about $0.999.

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:13.625
The margin: roughly 2.9% per coin.

00:07:13.625 --> 00:07:16.321
On paper, that margin should pull minters in

00:07:16.321 --> 00:07:18.838
and push the price back to $1.00, exactly

00:07:18.838 --> 00:07:20.875
like the loop you watched earlier.

00:07:22.175 --> 00:07:23.869
But notice exactly where you,

00:07:23.869 --> 00:07:25.975
personally, sit in this picture.

00:07:25.975 --> 00:07:30.510
You can't redeem directly, only verified institutional minters can.

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:32.835
So you're not doing the arbitrage yourself.

00:07:32.835 --> 00:07:35.006
You're relying on minters acting, and

00:07:35.006 --> 00:07:37.420
on the reserves genuinely being real.

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:40.479
If markets start doubting the reserves, minters may

00:07:40.479 --> 00:07:42.891
simply not step in, and the discount can

00:07:42.891 --> 00:07:45.833
grow instead of closing, no matter how attractive

00:07:45.833 --> 00:07:47.245
the math looks on paper.

00:07:48.545 --> 00:07:51.655
So here's the same habit, as a decision walk-through

00:07:51.655 --> 00:07:54.360
you can actually run during a real depeg.

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:56.025
First: check the type.

00:07:56.025 --> 00:07:59.475
Is it fiat, crypto, synthetic or algorithmic?

00:07:59.475 --> 00:08:01.250
Second: check redemption.

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:02.606
Can you, or can only

00:08:02.606 --> 00:08:05.610
institutions, actually redeem right now?

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:07.205
Third: check the news.

00:08:07.205 --> 00:08:09.856
Is this a reserve problem, or a market-wide

00:08:09.856 --> 00:08:12.385
panic with no reserve issue at all?

00:08:12.385 --> 00:08:15.121
And fourth: apply your exit rule, the one you

00:08:15.121 --> 00:08:18.095
set in advance, not one you invent under pressure.

00:08:19.395 --> 00:08:22.410
One honest note to close the worked example on.

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:25.555
None of this means avoid stablecoins entirely.

00:08:25.555 --> 00:08:27.652
They're genuinely useful, and you'll use

00:08:27.652 --> 00:08:29.845
them constantly through this program.

00:08:29.845 --> 00:08:33.094
It means treat “stable” as a description of intent,

00:08:33.094 --> 00:08:35.843
not a guarantee, and know which of the four

00:08:35.843 --> 00:08:39.591
designs you're actually holding before size or urgency ever

00:08:39.591 --> 00:08:42.215
makes that question hard to answer calmly.

00:08:43.515 --> 00:08:44.700
Last check.

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:47.715
What keeps a fiat-backed stablecoin trading near one dollar?

00:08:52.135 --> 00:08:55.153
The answer: redemption for a dollar of reserves,

00:08:55.153 --> 00:08:58.725
plus arbitrageurs who can actually access that redemption.

00:09:00.025 --> 00:09:01.967
Here's your checklist, as a habit

00:09:01.967 --> 00:09:03.980
for every stablecoin you hold.

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:06.175
Know the type and backing of each one.

00:09:06.175 --> 00:09:08.635
Know exactly who can redeem, and how.

00:09:08.635 --> 00:09:11.403
Don't count two stablecoins as diversified

00:09:11.403 --> 00:09:14.020
if they share issuers or collateral.

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:17.064
And set a depeg exit rule in advance, for

00:09:17.064 --> 00:09:20.544
example: sell or rotate out if it's below $0.99

00:09:20.544 --> 00:09:23.298
for longer than a set number of hours.

00:09:24.597 --> 00:09:25.842
Let's recap.

00:09:25.842 --> 00:09:28.276
Four designs, four different failure

00:09:28.276 --> 00:09:31.537
points: fiat, crypto, synthetic, algorithmic.

00:09:31.537 --> 00:09:33.710
A peg holds because of an arbitrage

00:09:33.710 --> 00:09:35.883
loop, and that loop only runs while

00:09:35.883 --> 00:09:38.417
redemption and reserves both hold up.

00:09:38.417 --> 00:09:41.072
Terra had neither, and spiralled to zero.

00:09:41.072 --> 00:09:44.087
U.S.D.C. had both, and recovered in days.

00:09:44.087 --> 00:09:46.916
Know your stablecoin's design, and have your exit

00:09:46.916 --> 00:09:48.897
rule ready before you ever need it.

00:09:50.197 --> 00:09:52.712
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:09:52.712 --> 00:09:54.927
You now have the whole lesson in your

00:09:54.927 --> 00:09:57.726
head: the four designs, the arbitrage loop that

00:09:57.726 --> 00:10:00.407
holds a peg together, two real collapses that

00:10:00.407 --> 00:10:04.022
ended completely differently, and a decision walk-through for

00:10:04.022 --> 00:10:06.362
the next time a stablecoin wobbles.

00:10:06.362 --> 00:10:09.417
Next up, Lesson 1.6: Scam defence.
